00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
when europeans turn on their radiators  or hit the light switch they expect  reliable and affordable energy to bring  them heat and light  that confidence is flickering as energy  prices soar  faced with calls for a bold response the  european union says it will examine the  way its power market is run and will  consider calls to revamp regulations  however the eu insists its plans to  tackle climate change must stay on track  winter is coming fears of the impending  temperature drop in the northern  hemisphere have driven gas prices to  record highs  since the start of the year prices have  surged at chilling 670 percent  spaniards are struggling to pay utility  bills and madrid wants the eu to make  group purchases of gas much as it did  for vaccinations for covet  greece wants an eu fund to help  governments weather the crisis  however other countries led by germany  and the netherlands urge caution  they argue the energy crunches short  term and related to the unprecedented  supply shock following the coronavirus  pandemic  some members fear the energy crisis  could lead to social unrest and should  take priority over combating climate  change  but the eu's climate chief franz  timmerman says there's no alternative  the levels of social unrest if we leave  the climate crisis untackled are going  to be unsupportable our children will  wage wars over water and food if we  don't tackle the climate crisis and of  course tackling the climate crisis also  leads to a number of difficult decisions  a number of big changes but let's keep  our eye on the ball the problem here is  the climate crisis and we need to act  quickly and europe needs to lead so that  the rest of the world understands where  we need to go  next week the european commission will  propose measures aimed at easing the  price surge for consumers  and making sure europe's kettles don't  go off the boil  and for more on this i'm joined by leon  izbiki he's a natural gas analyst with  energy aspects leon thank you for coming  on to the show um i want to start with  what happened yesterday when we saw  prices explode wholesale gas prices  explode what exactly happened  well i think you know that's a good  question ultimately the market has  become untethered because we have blown  through the traditional capsule to speak  on gas prices which are usually the gas  to coal switch and after that we have  also blown through the gas to liquid  switch so ultimately the market  was trying to find a level at which  there was further price resistance in  this case probably also from shedding  industrial demand for natural gas but i  think also it's important to highlight  that ultimately there is also a large  amount of speculation in the market and  this speculatory bubble really  was set to burst yesterday  remind us why are some countries more  exposed to these spot market rates than  others  so  ultimately this is a question of the  amount of stocks that you have in a  country and also  fundamentally really how much you could  actually fill up those stocks over the  last summer so the countries that we're  seeing particularly in northwest europe  uh having historically very low stocks  such as for example germany and austria  are essentially going to drive up that  price in the ttf in order to  further free up natural gas demand in  order to bring that into storage  you know member states are looking at  block purchases as a solution some of  them are we should say  is that a reasonable solution that the  eu does something with gas sort of like  what it did with vaccines this idea of a  purchase for everyone  uh i think it certainly is but i mean  the fundamental question is what  european policymakers can really do in  the current situation and fundamentally  also where they are going to purchase  that gas from because asia is showing no  signs of essentially losing the price  war with europe that is bidding for lng  cargoes and simultaneously there is also  this underlying question of whether  russia can actually increase flows to  europe so while it is  a good thing to think about  fundamentally purchasing gas on block  the question is where is that gas coming  from  there's been a lot of criticism that  russia hasn't delivered as much as it  needs to in a crisis um how do you see  it have you been able to look and see  what they have been delivering  well so fundamentally i think it's  important to highlight that ultimately  on a year-on-year level russian flows to  europe have increased but they are still  below  around 18 below pre-pandemic levels and  this happens in the context of where  we're actually seeing gas demand in  europe grow overall around three percent  compared to pre-pandemic levels so  russia has been meeting nominations on  the european gas market  but i mean fundamentally also  the the initial problem is  that russian production has increased  but a lot of that production actually  flows into the russian balance itself as  the country is looking to fill up its  law storage all right leon izbiki with  energy aspects thank you very much  thank you  all right dw correspondent in brussels  marina strauss joins me for more  marina good morning um so we hear that  the eu is encouraging member states to  provide relief to their citizens to try  and offset some of these rising prices  but is brussels itself prepared to do  anything  well in this case it's urging its member  states and sweden member states to help  small businesses and consumers because  these short-term measures is something  the single member state states have to  do themselves but in the longer term on  eu scale we saw that in the report some  eu member states for example france and  spain are asking for a change of the  rules that are governing the eu's energy  market for example spain  suggested this strategic gas reserve for  the whole of the eu  for emergencies  and wednesday next week the eu  commission will present a toolbox of  medium and long-term measures  so we have to wait a bit and see what  kind of ideas they come up with but this  clearly shows there is a political will  to tackle this issue here in brussels  and marina we also heard yesterday that  hungarian leader victor orban  made a connection between rising prices  energy prices and the rising cost of  carbon emissions in the eu the eu of  course covered by that emissions trading  scheme now those prices have risen  somewhat they're not as dramatically um  what do eu leaders say to that kind of  connection that he's putting there  i talked to franz timmermans  the commissioner or the vice president  of the eu commission who's responsible  for climate we saw in the report and he  told me yesterday that  according to the commission this is not  correct so he said that those measures  are only responsible to a very small  extent a maximum of one-fifth he said  and he claims that putting a price on  co2 emissions is not the problem but the  fact that the the market demands are on  an all-time high  due to eu economies coming out of the  covet 19 crisis and as other eu  politicians he says that the quicker the  eu switches to renewable energy sources  the quicker eu citizens are protected  from these uh energy spikes we saw in  the last couple of weeks  all right marina straus cw correspondent  in brussels thank you very much  now as we mentioned earlier european  countries are certainly not alone in  their struggles with energy supply india  is facing a crunch in coal following a  surge there in demand  now energy suppliers have recently added  over 28 million consumers in india  mostly lower and middle class and poor  who are buying goods like fans lights tv  sets  now it's not just soaring demand recent  monsoon rains flooded mines and  disrupted transport and that helped send  coal prices up sharply for power  stations  a coal generates nearly 70 percent of  india's electricity  three-quarters of which is mined  domestically  state-run giant coal india produces most  of the country's supply and says that  it's now on a war footing to ensure  deliveries  but supplies for more than half of  coal-fired plants are at a critical  level delhi says that it's even  considering rebooting idol power  stations now for more on this i'm joined  by dw correspondent charlo kartakai in  studio delhi achara good to see you um  how serious could this situation get for  businesses and families uh in india  good morning stephen it's an anxious  morning for uh officials of uh the power  ministry in india because uh the  situation appears to be very critical as  of now there are 135 coal powered power  plants that the regulatory body  monitors and out of these at least 16  have zero days of stock which means they  have completely run out of stock  on an average these plants have been  left with about four days of stock so  that's a pretty uh critical situation at  this point of time businesses are  definitely scared for households it's  not really uh uh they do not really  appear a bit perturbed because india  these kind of blackouts it's a very rare  phenomenon last time india had a  blackout it was in 2012. it was pretty  scary though because uh  about 400 million people were affected  on day one and another 600 million on  day two but that was uh nine years ago  and uh of course memories are still uh  very uh uh alive of that blackout but at  this point of time it is being ruled out  that we are heading towards that kind of  a situation again  char how is the indian press looking at  this for example is this an issue do  they see an issue here about energy  diversification in india did they see it  about green policies did they see this  uh relating to the pandemic how's it  being handled there  it's all these things combined steven  one is of course uh the pandemic had led  to a fall in demand so probably uh the  authorities had gotten a bit uh like a  daisical about how to manage the supply  because post pandemic there's been a  surge in demand even though industrial  activity is yet to pick up so this is  mostly household demand as you rightly  mentioned in the introduction that 28  million households have got new  connections so the consumption has  definitely  gone up but of course yes this is a  problem with india's energy mix as you  rightly said some estimates suggest that  coal accounts for about 70 percent of  india's energy mix  we've been trying to go ahead with solar  and increase our dependence on solar but  that hasn't been working out very well  there are some estimates suggest that uh  this month the uh the the growth in  solar output has fallen by about 40  percent as compared to uh on a  year-on-year basis so solar is not  really achieving its potential and which  is further increasing our uh  dependence on coal  all right but many accounts a bad year  for renewables character in delhi thank  you very much

